## Slide Types (token_saver mode only)

cover, content, stats, list, steps, comparison, quote, code, cta, timeline, team

## Output Formats

| Format | Notes |
|--------|-------|
| pdf | Default. Best for LinkedIn carousels, sharing, printing. |
| png | High-quality images, universal compatibility. |
| webp | Lightweight images for web/social (95% quality default). |
| pptx | PowerPoint file. **Image mode (default)** preserves design but is NOT editable. **Native mode** (opt-in) extracts text only — design WILL NOT be preserved (looks broken in PowerPoint). For truly editable decks, use PDF or recreate in PowerPoint. |

## Key Rules

- **Use mode=default** unless user explicitly chose token_saver
- **render_slides bypasses gates** when htmlPath points to an existing file on disk
- **AI decides slide count** — do NOT ask the user how many slides
- **No duplicate display** — use native selectors only, do NOT render themes as markdown list
- **Data outline before HTML** — show bullet-point outline and get user approval before generating
- **Present outlineConfirm selector** after the outline for quick approval
- Always use `htmlPath` from create_slides for render_slides
- Default output format is PDF
- Default orientation is portrait (540×675)
- Default scale is 4x
- Default `pptxMode` is `"image"` — pixel-perfect, design preserved, text NOT editable
- `pptxMode: "native"` is opt-in for text-only export and emits a warning that design won't be preserved
- Output goes to ~/Desktop/slideshot-output (or fallback locations)
- **NEVER skip the preview step** — always show HTML as code preview artifact before rendering
- **NEVER call render_slides in the same turn as create_slides**
